# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 001
ps("V02-M-001", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Forgetting forces the memory theory of identity into a repair that concedes identity rests on wider continuity.",
   "Easy",
   "A popular answer to the question of what makes someone the same person over time is memory: I am the person who did the things I can remember doing. The view has real appeal, since it explains why amnesia seems to threaten the self in a way that losing a limb does not. But it struggles with ordinary forgetting. I remember nothing of being two years old, yet I do not think the toddler in the old photographs was someone else. Defenders reply that a chain of overlapping memories, each linking to the one before, is enough. That repair helps, but it quietly concedes the original point: it is not remembering that makes me the same person, but something continuous of which remembering is only one part.",
   "The memory account of identity appeals, but ordinary forgetting forces a repair conceding that identity rests on a wider continuity, of which memory is only one strand.",
   [("Because adults cannot remember their earliest years, the memory account wrongly implies that each of us was once a quite different person from the one we are now.",
     "This states only the objection from forgetting; it leaves out the reply about overlapping memories and the conclusion that identity rests on 'something continuous of which remembering is only one part'."),
    ("Memory plays no real part in what makes a person the same over time, as the cases of amnesia and of childhood forgetting both clearly demonstrate to us.",
     "The paragraph says memory is 'only one part' of what makes me the same person, not no part, and it uses amnesia to show the view's appeal, not to refute it."),
    ("A chain of overlapping memories, each linked to the one before it, fully rescues the memory account from the objection that people forget their early childhood.",
     "The paragraph says the repair 'helps' but 'quietly concedes the original point'; saying it fully rescues the account reverses the author's verdict.")],
   "The paragraph states the memory view, grants its appeal, raises the problem of forgetting, considers the overlapping-chain reply and concludes that the reply gives the game away: identity depends on a broader continuity, with memory 'only one part'. The correct answer keeps all three steps and the final qualification.",
   "Picking the option that restates the forgetting objection and missing the final turn, or treating the repair as a success.",
   "V02M-memory-and-personal-identity",
   "The argument turns once, in the last sentence, and each wrong option misstates a single clearly signposted step.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 002
ps("V02-M-002", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Arguments about taste cannot compel enjoyment but are worthwhile because they redirect attention.",
   "Medium",
   "It is often said that there is no disputing about taste, and in one sense this is right: no argument will make someone enjoy a dish they find bitter. Yet people argue about taste constantly, and not pointlessly. When a friend explains what to listen for in a piece of music, or why a film's slow opening earns its later payoff, she is not trying to overrule my pleasure by decree. She is directing my attention to features I may have missed, so that my next response is better informed. Such arguments cannot compel agreement, but they can change what there is to respond to. Taste is not beyond reason; it is simply reached by reasons of a different kind, ones that work by showing rather than by proving.",
   "Argument cannot force anyone to enjoy something, yet disputes about taste remain worthwhile, because they draw attention to overlooked features and persuade by showing rather than by proof.",
   [("Since no argument can make anyone enjoy what they dislike, discussions about music or film are pointless exchanges of preference that reason is unable to touch.",
     "The paragraph says people argue about taste 'not pointlessly' and that taste 'is not beyond reason', so this reverses its conclusion."),
    ("Well-informed critics can, with enough patient explanation, eventually bring other people round to agreeing with their judgements about which films and music are good.",
     "The paragraph says such arguments 'cannot compel agreement'; their value lies in informing a response, not in producing eventual agreement with the critic."),
    ("A slow opening in a film can be justified by the payoff it earns later, which an attentive viewer gradually learns to notice and to appreciate.",
     "This is one of the paragraph's examples; it leaves out the general claim about arguing over taste and the distinction between showing and proving.")],
   "The paragraph concedes that argument cannot compel enjoyment, then argues that aesthetic disputes still have a point: they direct attention to features one missed, working 'by showing rather than by proving'. The correct answer keeps the concession and the main claim.",
   "Choosing the option about critics winning agreement, which sounds like a defence of aesthetic argument but contradicts 'cannot compel agreement'.",
   "V02M-disputes-about-taste",
   "One wrong option defends argument about taste in the wrong way (as producing agreement), so the reader must notice how the paragraph limits what such argument achieves.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 003
ps("V02-M-003", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Respecting choices is often evidence of a person's welfare, not merely a price paid for liberty.",
   "Difficult",
   "Critics of paternalism usually frame the issue as a contest between welfare and freedom: the state may make you better off, but only by overriding your choices. This framing concedes too much to the paternalist and too little to the person. It assumes that we already know what is good for someone independently of what they would choose, so that the only question is whether to impose it. In many cases we do not. Whether a risky career, a strict diet or a dangerous hobby serves a person's welfare depends partly on the plans and commitments that give their life its shape, and these are often visible only in their choices. Respecting someone's decisions is therefore not merely a price paid for liberty; it is frequently our best evidence of what their welfare actually is.",
   "The welfare-versus-freedom contest misleads: what benefits people often depends on their own commitments, so their choices frequently reveal their welfare rather than compete with it.",
   [("Since only individuals can know what is good for them, any attempt by the state to protect people from their own choices is bound to leave them worse off.",
     "The paragraph hedges throughout ('In many cases', 'often', 'frequently'); it does not claim that only the individual can know their good or that every protective measure fails."),
    ("Paternalism forces a hard choice between making people better off and leaving them free, and its critics rightly hold that freedom should usually win that contest.",
     "This is the framing the author rejects in the second sentence as conceding 'too much to the paternalist', so it presents the criticised view as the author's own."),
    ("Respecting people's decisions matters chiefly because liberty is a value weighty enough to justify the losses in welfare that free choices sometimes bring about.",
     "The last sentence says respecting decisions is 'not merely a price paid for liberty'; treating it as a price paid in welfare is exactly what the author denies.")],
   "The author rejects the welfare-versus-freedom framing because it assumes welfare can be known apart from choice. Since a person's good often depends on their own plans, which show up in their choices, respecting those choices is often evidence about welfare. The correct answer keeps the rejection, the reason and the hedge ('frequently').",
   "Choosing the liberty-as-a-price option, which defends autonomy in the conventional way the paragraph sets out to replace.",
   "V02M-paternalism-and-autonomy",
   "Two wrong options state familiar anti-paternalist positions; only a reader who sees that the author rejects the trade-off framing itself can separate them from the correct answer.",
   exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 004
ps("V02-M-004", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Relativism cannot ground tolerance because urging tolerance everywhere assumes a universal standard.",
   "Medium",
   "Moral relativism is often defended as the natural ally of tolerance: if no culture's values are truer than another's, no one has grounds to impose their own. The alliance is less secure than it looks. If all values are merely local, then tolerance itself is just one local value among others, with no more claim on a culture that rejects it than the custom of eating with chopsticks has on a culture that uses forks. The relativist who tells an intolerant society that it ought to be tolerant is appealing to exactly the kind of cross-cultural standard that relativism denies. Tolerance, it seems, needs at least one value that holds everywhere; it cannot rest on the claim that none does.",
   "Relativism cannot support tolerance as its defenders hope, because urging tolerance on every society presupposes the very universal standard that relativism says does not exist.",
   [("Since relativism undermines tolerance, every society should adopt a single universal moral code and require all cultures to live by its rules and values.",
     "The paragraph claims only that tolerance needs 'at least one value that holds everywhere'; it proposes no single code and no enforcement on all cultures."),
    ("Because no culture's values are truer than another's, every society has good reason to tolerate practices that differ from its own customs and beliefs.",
     "This is the relativist defence of tolerance that the author says is 'less secure than it looks', so it reverses the author's position."),
    ("Tolerance is a merely local value, comparable to a custom such as eating with chopsticks, and so it carries no special weight for societies that reject it.",
     "This describes what tolerance would be if relativism were true, a consequence the author uses to attack relativism, not a view the author holds.")],
   "The paragraph argues that relativism undercuts the tolerance it is meant to support: if all values are local, so is tolerance, and urging it on others appeals to a 'cross-cultural standard'. The correct answer states this self-defeat and keeps the scope (it is about grounding tolerance).",
   "Taking the chopsticks comparison, which describes the relativist's predicament, as the author's own claim about tolerance.",
   "V02M-relativism-and-tolerance",
   "One wrong option repeats the paragraph's own words about tolerance being local, and the reader must see that it is a reductio, not a conclusion.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 005
ps("V02-M-005", "Psychology",
   "Expert intuition is reliable only where environments are regular and feedback is quick.",
   "Easy",
   "Experienced firefighters, chess players and nurses often make good decisions in seconds without being able to say why. It is tempting to conclude that intuition is a general gift that grows with years in any job. But intuition is learned pattern recognition, and it can only be learned where there are patterns to recognise and where mistakes are quickly revealed. A chess position either loses or it does not, and the player soon finds out. A long-range political forecaster or a stock picker works in a world that is noisy and slow to give feedback, and years of experience there can produce confidence without producing skill.",
   "Intuition is learned pattern recognition, so it becomes reliable only where situations are regular and mistakes show up quickly; elsewhere long experience may breed confidence without skill.",
   [("Intuition cannot be trusted in any profession, because even highly experienced experts who decide quickly are seldom able to explain the reasons behind their judgements.",
     "The paragraph opens by saying firefighters, chess players and nurses 'often make good decisions' by intuition, so it does not distrust intuition everywhere."),
    ("Intuition is a general gift that steadily improves with years of experience, which explains why seasoned professionals in every field, from nursing to finance, decide well.",
     "This is the conclusion the paragraph calls 'tempting' and then rejects with 'But'; the stock picker example shows experience need not bring skill."),
    ("Chess players develop dependable intuitions because every position either loses or does not, so players quickly discover which of their moves were mistakes.",
     "This is only the supporting example; it omits the general condition and the contrast with forecasters and stock pickers.")],
   "The paragraph rejects the idea that intuition grows with experience in any job and states the condition for it: patterns to learn and quick feedback on mistakes. Chess meets the condition; forecasting does not. The correct answer gives the condition and the consequence where it fails.",
   "Choosing the 'general gift' option because the paragraph begins by praising intuitive experts.",
   "V02M-expert-intuition-reliability",
   "The turn is marked by 'But' and the wrong options are an overstatement, the rejected view and a single example.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 006
ps("V02-M-006", "Psychology",
   "Bystanders in crowds help less because of misread cues and diffused responsibility, not callousness.",
   "Medium",
   "When someone collapses on a crowded street, each passer-by is less likely to help than a person who comes upon the same victim alone. This is often read as evidence that crowds make people callous, but the mechanism looks less like indifference than like confusion. In a crowd, everyone checks everyone else's face for cues, and since each person is trying to look calm, the crowd as a whole seems to signal that nothing is wrong. Responsibility is also spread thinly: if many people could act, no one feels that the duty is specifically theirs. Where a single onlooker is singled out, say by being pointed at and asked to call for help, the effect largely disappears.",
   "People in crowds help less not chiefly from indifference but because they misread one another's calm and feel responsibility spread thin; singling one out largely restores help.",
   [("Crowds tend to make individuals callous towards strangers, so a victim who collapses in a busy street is less likely to get help than one who is found alone.",
     "The paragraph mentions this reading only to reject it: the mechanism 'looks less like indifference than like confusion'."),
    ("Anyone who collapses in a public place ought to point at one particular onlooker and ask that person for help, rather than appealing to the crowd in general.",
     "The paragraph explains why help fails and notes what removes the effect; it gives no advice to victims, and this option drops the explanation, which is its main point."),
    ("Passers-by in a crowd deliberately pretend to stay calm so that they can avoid the trouble of helping, leaving each victim to be rescued by somebody else.",
     "The paragraph says people try to look calm and so mislead each other; it does not say they feign calm in order to avoid helping, which would bring back the callousness it rejects.")],
   "The paragraph rejects the callousness explanation and gives two mechanisms, misread cues and diffused responsibility, then supports them with the observation that singling someone out largely removes the effect. The correct answer carries the rejection, both mechanisms and the evidence.",
   "Choosing the 'deliberately pretend' option, which uses the paragraph's point about calm faces but turns confusion into evasion.",
   "V02M-bystander-diffusion-of-responsibility",
   "One wrong option reuses the calm-face detail but changes its motive, and another turns the closing observation into a prescription.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 007
ps("V02-M-007", "Psychology",
   "Waiting for a bigger reward may also reflect learned trust in promises, not only self-control.",
   "Difficult",
   "Children who can wait for a larger treat rather than take a smaller one at once have long been held up as models of self-control, and their patience has been linked to later success. Yet waiting is rational only if the promised reward is likely to arrive. A child whose adults have reliably kept their word has good reason to wait; a child whose promises have often been broken has equally good reason to take what is on the table. In versions of the test where an adult first breaks a small promise to the child, waiting times tend to fall. None of this shows that self-control is unimportant. It does suggest that a measure taken to reveal a trait of character may also be recording what the child has learned about the world.",
   "A child's readiness to wait for a larger reward may partly reflect a sensible judgement about whether promises are kept, not only self-control, which still matters.",
   [("Children who learn to trust adults become more patient, so parents who reliably keep their promises will raise children who go on to succeed in later life.",
     "The paragraph links trust to waiting in the test, but it never claims that trust produces later success; the chain to adult achievement is added."),
    ("Patience in the waiting test is better explained by a child's rational trust than by self-control, so the test's links with later success are probably illusory.",
     "The paragraph says the test 'may also be recording' learned trust and that self-control is not 'unimportant'; ranking trust above self-control and dismissing the links goes beyond it."),
    ("Children who can wait for a larger treat rather than accept a smaller one at once tend to be more successful in later life than those who cannot.",
     "This restates the finding in the first sentence and leaves out the paragraph's qualification, which is its point.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding (waiting is linked to later success), then qualifies how it is read: waiting is rational only if rewards arrive, and broken promises reduce waiting. It explicitly keeps self-control in the picture. The correct answer gives the qualification at the right strength (partly, not only).",
   "Choosing the 'better explained by a child's rational trust' option, which captures the direction of the argument but overstates its strength.",
   "V02M-delayed-gratification-and-trust",
   "The correct answer and the strongest wrong option differ only in degree ('may partly reflect' versus 'better explained'), so the reader must track the paragraph's hedges.",
   exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 008
ps("V02-M-008", "Psychology",
   "The fuel-tank model of willpower is weaker than its fame; beliefs about effort may matter.",
   "Easy",
   "A popular idea holds that willpower is like a fuel tank: every decision drains it, so that by evening we are too depleted to resist temptation. The image is vivid, but the evidence for a simple reservoir has proved weaker than its fame suggests, and attempts to repeat some of the best-known experiments have produced smaller effects or none. Some studies suggest instead that expectations matter. People who believe that effort exhausts them tend to flag after demanding tasks, while those who see effort as energising flag less. Tiredness after a long day is real, but how much it governs our choices may depend partly on the story we tell ourselves about it.",
   "The notion that each decision drains a fixed store of willpower is weaker than its fame suggests; our depletion may depend partly on beliefs about effort.",
   [("Because each decision drains a limited store of willpower, people should make their most important choices early in the day, before that reserve runs low.",
     "The paragraph casts doubt on the fuel-tank model, so advice built on it reverses the paragraph's point."),
    ("Tiredness after a long working day is largely imaginary, and people who simply stop believing in it will find that effort no longer affects their self-control.",
     "The paragraph says 'Tiredness after a long day is real' and that beliefs matter only 'partly', so this overstates and contradicts it."),
    ("Several of the best-known experiments on willpower have since been repeated by other researchers, and the repetitions have generally found smaller effects or none.",
     "This is one piece of evidence; it leaves out the alternative account based on expectations and the closing qualification.")],
   "The paragraph doubts the fuel-tank model, offers expectations as a partial alternative and keeps a qualification: tiredness is real. The correct answer captures the doubt and the hedged alternative.",
   "Choosing the 'largely imaginary' option, which takes the role of belief too far.",
   "V02M-decision-fatigue-willpower",
   "The wrong options are a reversal, an exaggeration contradicted by one sentence, and a single detail.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 009
ps("V02-M-009", "Society and culture",
   "Small talk lets strangers safely test each other's goodwill; judging it by information misses its purpose.",
   "Medium",
   "Small talk is usually dismissed as the emptiest form of conversation: remarks about the weather or the traffic that convey nothing anyone did not already know. But to judge it by its information content is to mistake its purpose. When strangers exchange a few safe sentences, they are testing whether the other is friendly, cooperative and willing to follow shared rules, at almost no risk to either. The very predictability of the topics is what makes this possible, since a remark nobody could disagree with leaves room only for tone. Small talk is not a failed attempt at real conversation; it is often the means by which strangers decide whether to attempt one.",
   "Small talk seems empty only when judged by the information it carries; its predictable topics let strangers safely gauge each other's goodwill before they risk a deeper conversation.",
   [("Remarks about the weather and the traffic are popular among strangers because nobody could reasonably disagree with them or take offence at what they say.",
     "This keeps the detail about safe topics but drops the paragraph's claim about what small talk is for, testing goodwill before real conversation."),
    ("People ought to value small talk above serious conversation, since predictable remarks on safe subjects reveal far more about character than any discussion of ideas.",
     "The paragraph ranks nothing above serious conversation and gives no advice; it presents small talk as a step towards 'real conversation', not a superior substitute."),
    ("Small talk is more informative than it seems, because remarks about the weather and traffic often tell strangers useful facts that they did not already know.",
     "The paragraph accepts that such remarks convey 'nothing anyone did not already know'; its point is that information is the wrong measure.")],
   "The paragraph rejects judging small talk by its information and gives its real function: a low-risk test of the other person's goodwill, made possible by predictable topics, that often precedes real conversation. The correct answer states the rejection and the function.",
   "Choosing the 'more informative' option, which defends small talk but on the very measure the paragraph says is mistaken.",
   "V02M-small-talk-social-function",
   "Two wrong options defend small talk, one by distorting its value and one by overstating it, so the reader must hold onto the exact function claimed.")

